1x1 controller 31 (engineering-practical guide)
 below is a compact, engineering-practical guide for how much capacitance (and what protection/passive parts) to add around your H-bridges, plus worked example calculations for your three battery cases. I’ll give rules-of-thumb, formulas you can use to refine values, concrete example numbers, and recommended placement/topology and part types (including snubbers, TVS, pre-charge, brake resistor, etc.). Use this as a blueprint to drop into your Colab / BOM / hardware notes.

High-level design goals
    1. Stabilize DC bus voltage during high current transients (prevent large ΔV when motors draw pulsed current).
    2. Absorb / buffer regenerative energy (or provide a safe path: battery, supercap, or brake resistor).
    3. Suppress switching spikes & EMI (local decoupling, snubbers, common-mode chokes).
    4. Protect power electronics (TVS, RCD clamps, pre-charge, fuses/contactors).
    5. Keep weight/cost in check — avoid trying to store large regenerative energy in DC bus caps unless you explicitly want very large supercap banks.

Important formulas (use these to size caps)
    1. Capacitor needed to hold voltage during a current pulse
[
C = \frac{I \cdot \Delta t}{\Delta V}
]
where
• (I) = peak transient current (A) drawn from DC bus,
• (\Delta t) = duration of the pulse (s),
• (\Delta V) = allowable DC bus droop (V).
    2. Capacitor to absorb energy (E) from regeneration
[
C = \frac{2E}{V_{\text{max}}^{2} - V_{\text{min}}^{2}}
]
where (E) is the regen energy (J). (V_{max}) and (V_{min}) are the bus voltages at the extremes you allow when absorbing the energy.
    3. Practical derating
Account for ESR heating, temperature derating and aging. Multiply calculated C by a safety factor 1.5–3 depending on risk tolerance.
    4. Power delivered by sudden ΔV
[
E_{\text{cap}} = \tfrac{1}{2} C (V_{1}^2 - V_{2}^2)
]
useful to check how much energy a proposed C can absorb before hitting voltage limits.

Placement & topology (where to put what)
    • Before H-bridge (DC bus / bulk cap)
Bulk DC-link: large electrolytic / polymer capacitor bank (tens of mF up to farads depending on requirements). Purpose: buffer large current pulses and handle bulk energy. Use a hybrid: electrolytic (or polymer electrolytic) + film caps in parallel for lower ESR.
    • Right at H-bridge / switch node (local decoupling)
Ceramics/MLCCs (0.1–10 µF) and small film caps (100 nF–1 µF) placed close to MOSFETs to suppress high-frequency switching transients.
    • Across motor phases (motor-side EMI caps)
Small Y or X class EMI capacitors or phase-to-phase ceramics (10 nF–100 nF) can reduce EMI. Avoid large capacitance to earth unless you manage leakage currents and standards.
    • Across each coil / phase snubber
R-C snubber (e.g., 47–470 nF with series resistor 5–100 Ω) or RCD clamp if you want to absorb spike energy. For hard switching, RCD clamp is preferred.
    • Gate driver & bootstrap
Local 0.1–1.0 µF ceramics for bootstrap; 10–47 µF tantalum/polymer for local gate-driver supply stabilization.
    • Regeneration path
Battery can accept regen (preferred). If not, use a brake resistor + MOSFET switch or active dump circuit. If you want to store regen on bus caps, see energy calc below.
    • Pre-charge & inrush
Add pre-charge resistor and contactor or an NTC + bypass contactor. Prevents capacitor charging surge.

Protection devices
    • TVS diode(s) across DC bus sized to clamp slightly above max DC bus voltage (fast transient suppression). Pick low-inductance TVS with appropriate standoff voltage.
    • MOV for high-energy surge protection (if mains connected).
    • RCD snubber or RC across MOSFETs for dv/dt/dV spikes.
    • Brake resistor (sized to dissipate worst-case regen energy), with an FET to connect it when bus voltage rises.
    • Contactors / fuses / fast-blow breakers sized for continuous and transient currents.
    • Common-mode choke + Y capacitors for EMI if motor leads to long cable run.
    • High-speed freewheeling diodes typically unnecessary if using modern MOSFETs (intrinsic body diodes exist) — but for IGBT-based drives consider external fast diodes or snubbers.

Practical sizing examples — use as starting points
Assume you want bus voltage sag limited to ΔV ~ 5 V (48 V system) or ΔV ~ 10 V (higher-voltage systems) for short transients.
Example method 1 — transient current pulse sizing
Choose conservative pulse: dt = 1 ms (typical brief torque pulse), but pick dt per your expected worst-case current profile.
Battery case A — 48 V system (B1)
    • Peak current (I = 100) A (peak), dt = 1 ms, allowable ΔV = 5 V:
[
C = \frac{100 \cdot 0.001}{5} = 0.02\ \text{F} = 20\ \text{mF}
]
Use safety factor 2 → 40 mF. Choose low-ESR polymer electrolytic or a combination: e.g., 2×22 mF polymer caps (in parallel) + film cap(s).
Battery case B — 96 V system (B2)
    • I = 250 A, dt = 1 ms, ΔV = 5 V:
[
C = \frac{250 \cdot 0.001}{5} = 0.05\ \text{F} = 50\ \text{mF}
]
With safety margin → 100 mF (or split into several caps to reduce ESR).
Battery case C — EV nominal (B3) — say 400 V bus (typical)
    • I = 500 A, dt = 1 ms, ΔV = 10 V:
[
C = \frac{500 \cdot 0.001}{10} = 0.05\ \text{F} = 50\ \text{mF}
]
Safety factor 2 → 100 mF (but at 400 V rating this becomes very large/expensive — you’ll likely rely on battery absorbing pulse).
Notes: these values are for short pulses only. If your pulses are longer (10 ms, 100 ms), required C scales linearly.

Example method 2 — regen energy absorption (shows why huge caps are impractical)
Suppose a regen event: 5 kW for 0.1 s → (E = 500\ \text{J}).
For 48 V system, choose clamp range from 54 V down to 42 V (Vmax^2 − Vmin^2 = 2916 − 1764 = 1152)
[
C = \frac{2 \cdot 500}{1152} \approx 0.87\ \text{F}
]
→ ~0.9 F at low voltage — that's a very large capacitor bank. At higher voltage rails the required capacitance reduces per the V^2 term but still large and costly. Conclusion: don’t rely on DC caps to soak significant regen unless you plan big supercap banks.
Practical implication: use battery + regen resistor + active braking instead of huge caps.

Concrete recommended components / layout (practical)
Bulk DC link (before H-bridge)
    • 48 V case: 40–100 mF total low-ESR polymer electrolytic capacitors rated ≥ 63 V (use margin). Add 1–2 µF film or multiple MLCCs in parallel for HF decoupling.
    • 96 V case: 50–200 mF polymer caps rated ≥150 V (or multiple lower-voltage caps in series with balancing resistors). Consider film cap bank (safer at higher energy).
    • 400 V case: Use smaller capacitance but higher voltage film caps — aim for 10–100 mF total is expensive; often rely on battery for buffering and keep DC-link smaller (few mF) plus braking/resistor.
Local decoupling (right at MOSFETs)
    • 0.1–1 µF MLCC (X7R), multiple in parallel for lower ESR/ESL.
    • 100 nF film (polypropylene) for snubbing.
Phase snubbers
    • RCD clamp or RC snubber per half-bridge: example 100 nF + 10 Ω (tune to your switching energy).
    • For MOSFET switching nodes, add 1–4 µH common-mode choke + small Y caps for EMI.
TVS & surge
    • TVS rated to clamp at ~Vbus + 10–15%. Choose surge energy rating consistent with worst-case spike.
Brake resistor & dump
    • Calculate resistor R such that P_dissipated manageable and peak voltage limited; add FET to connect resistor when bus > threshold.
Inrush / pre-charge
    • 10–100 Ω pre-charge resistor sized for inrush current, with contactor to bypass after charge.
Fuses/contactors
    • Fast blow / DC rated fuses sized for continuous operating current with margin and peak capabilities.

Practical BOM examples (starting point)
(Select per bus voltage; these are illustrative part-scale choices.)
48 V bus example
    • Bulk caps: 2 × 22 mF, 63 V polymer electrolytic (in parallel → 44 mF).
    • Film caps: 2 × 1 µF polypropylene across the bus for HF suppression.
    • Local MLCCs: 4 × 1 µF MLCC per H-bridge module.
    • TVS: SMBJ58CA (choose part rated around 58–60 V standoff, clamp 90 V) — pick appropriate power rating.
    • RCD snubber per half-bridge: 100 nF / 10 Ω (1 W resistor or higher depending on energy).
    • Brake resistor + MOSFET: sized to absorb regen energy in bursts.
    • Pre-charge resistor 10–47 Ω, 5–10 W.
96 V bus example
    • Bulk: 4 × 22 mF, 100 V polymer caps (paralleled) → 88 mF.
    • Film caps: 2 × 2.2 µF polypropylene.
    • TVS: choose 110–120 V standoff, power rating accordingly.
    • Snubbers: 100 nF / 10 Ω RCD or heavier if switching energy high.
400 V EV-like bus
    • Bulk: several film capacitors (e.g., 10–47 µF film caps at 450 VAC rating) or smaller electrolytic+film hybrid totaling some µF to low-mF — because large mF at 400 V is very heavy/expensive.
    • Use battery to absorb regen or dedicated supercap module if you need buffering.

How to choose Δt, ΔV, and regen policy (recommended workflow)
    1. Identify worst-case current pulse (I_peak, duration) from motor control / controller behavior (simulate or measure). Use crest factor and switching profiles.
    2. Pick allowable bus droop ΔV (e.g., 2–5% of nominal bus voltage for stable control). Smaller ΔV → larger C.
    3. Compute C = I·Δt/ΔV. Apply safety factor 2.
    4. Estimate regen energy in a plausible event (e.g., braking from X rpm to Y rpm or current regeneration waveform). Use C = 2E/(V^2diff) if you intend to soak energy in caps. If C > ~0.1–1 F (for low-voltage systems), use brake resistor or send energy to battery.
    5. Size TVS and snubbers for transient clamp and absorbed energy.
    6. Choose physical caps with low ESR and suitable ripple current rating and temperature rating. Prefer polymers or film for long life.

Additional controller-side components to consider
    • Bulk cap balancing resistors (if using series caps).
    • ESR/ESL selection — lower ESR reduces voltage sag but increases inrush — use pre-charge.
    • Current sense resistor + RC filter, isolation amplifier for ADC.
    • Gate driver decoupling and isolated DC-DC for gate drivers if high-side floating.
    • Bootstrap diode & cap 0.1–1 µF ceramic (plus 4.7–10 µF electrolytic on gate driver supply).

Short checklist / design recipe you can run now
    1. Choose I_peak and dt for your worst pulses (e.g., 100–300 A peak, 1–10 ms).
    2. Choose allowable ΔV (2–5% of bus).
    3. Compute C = I·dt/ΔV and multiply by 2 safety factor.
    4. Check regen energy E and compute C for regen absorption; if C too large, plan regen to battery or brake resistor.
    5. Pick caps: split bulk into multiple capacitors (reduces ESR, helps layout) and add MLCCs across H-bridge.
    6. Add TVS (bus clamp), RCD snubber per half-bridge and pre-charge resistor + contactor.
    7. Add EMI filter (CMC + Y caps) if needed for EMC.
    8. Prototype with simulated worst-case pulses and verify ΔV, temperatures, and capacitor ripple heating. Adjust caps and snubbers as needed.

